From 9304fdde76e0af164dad891816bb8cf14da9d726 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vishal Kumar Swain <vkswain50@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 22:04:16 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] neurosym: serve counterexamples from NeuroSym's own model
 output

The --neurosym-model-prog side solver was always waited on for every
counterexample, even though NeuroSym already prints a sort-correct
SMT-LIB2 model block on its own sat verdict. On a real captured
40MB/998-VCC formula the side solver (an interactive z3 -in session over
the same formula) took as long on its own as NeuroSym's whole batch solve
-- the model was being built twice, sequentially.

oneshot_process::run_solver() gains an optional captured_output
out-parameter to keep the solver's full stdout, not just the small
diagnostic tail already kept. neurosym_convt::parse_model_block() parses
NeuroSym's define-fun model block from it into local_model, and
get_bv()/l_get()/get_bool() serve straight from that map by symname
before ever touching the side solver.

The wait on --neurosym-model-prog is now lazy (ensure_model_prog_ready(),
gated on first actual miss) rather than unconditional in dec_solve():
when every queried symbol is a plain declared variable covered by
local_model -- the common case, and the only case for QF_BV/QF_ABV
formulas without derived array/pointer values in the trace -- the side
solver is started (still needed as a hot-start fallback) but its answer
is never waited for. Validated: a small QF_BV case built a correct,
verified counterexample in 0.49s with the side solver never queried at
all. A composite/derived-expression value (an empty smt_astt symname --
not a plain variable) still falls back to it lazily, the first time one
is actually needed; local_model cannot evaluate expressions, only look
up declared symbols, so this is an intentional, safe fallback boundary,
not a gap that was missed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
